Detroit Golf Club in 2026 is a renovated, medium‑length Donald Ross par‑70 that still yields low scores, but now rewards modern driving, high‑end tee‑to‑green and real short‑game competence more than past birdie‑fest editions.

Venue & Renovation Snapshot
Designer: Donald Ross (1916) with a full Tyler Rae renovation completed ahead of the 2025–26 Rocket Classic cycle; Andrew Green’s prior work is also referenced in the current routing/spec.

New tournament setup: Par‑72 ~7,370 has been rebuilt into Par‑70 7,328 yards; holes 4 (592) and 14 (561) are now the only par‑5s, with the old par‑5 7th and 17th converted to long par‑4s at 505 and 537 yards.

Greens: All 18 greens rebuilt to USGA spec, fresh bentgrass (no Poa), expanded dramatically from ~5,170 sq ft to ~6,703 sq ft average, with restored Ross edge contours and pin positions lost over decades. New surfaces tend to repel approach shots in year one, which should temper scoring.

Fairways/bunkers: Fairway acreage up ~2 acres overall but effective landing‑area width down from ~30 to 26 yards at 300 yards; bunkers increased from 83 to 103, with substantial reshaping around greens to restore strategy.

Routing/modernization: Select tees moved to match modern driving, trees selectively removed to reintroduce Ross sightlines; drainage and irrigation modernized, intent is more thoughtful shot‑making rather than pure bash‑and‑wedge.

Mechanism: Detroit is still a low‑scoring Ross parkland, but the renovation has tightened driving corridors, toughened approach into new greens, and added more meaningful hazard placement around modern distances.

Course Specs & Hole‑Level Structure
High‑level tournament specs:

Par: 70

Yardage: 7,328

Water in play: 0 holes

Bunkers: 103

Fairway acreage: 32 acres

Fairway width: 26 yards at 300 yards (tour‑narrow relative to most recent birdie‑fest venues).

Surface profile:

Fairways: Bentgrass

Rough: Tall fescue / bluegrass mix, listed at 4"

Greens: Pure bentgrass, ~6,703 sq ft average

Tournament stimp: target ~11

Structural notes by bucket (using Bamford’s description plus renovation logic):

Par‑5s (4, 14):

4: ~592 yards with OB right and a major tree on the left, explicitly described by Potgieter as a grind tee shot where he cannot just start it left and fade; this is a pressure scoring hole that punishes loose tee balls and shape‑indifferent drivers.

14: ~561 yards, still reachable for top‑end length; tour quotes repeatedly emphasize “reachable par‑5s” as core scoring engines.

New long par‑4s (7 ~505, 17 ~537):

Functionally play like former par‑5 approach patterns: long‑iron / hybrid in, heavy premium on high‑speed ball‑striking and long‑approach competence; these are pressure holes in the new DNA, particularly under weekend wind or cross‑breezes.

Typical Ross par‑4s (mid‑length):

Uneven lies, tree‑lined corridors, and greens with front‑to‑back tilt and tiers, often requiring shaping even from fairway to find proper tiers; Davis explicitly notes having to bend shots around trees from fairway.

Par‑3s:

Not individually specified, but Ross + expanded greens + more bunkering implies a mix of elevated and contour‑heavy targets, where missing on the wrong side severely stresses short game.

Pressure‑hole concept:

Front‑nine: Potgieter explicitly frames 1–4 as a grind stretch, including a disliked tee shot on 2 and the demanding 4th with OB/tree framing; early round stability is a structural skill here.

Back‑nine: 14 and 17 together build a par‑5 followed quickly by a 530+ par‑4; this is a critical momentum cluster where bombers can separate and weak long‑approach players hemorrhage strokes.

