Driving Corridors & Par‑5 Reachability
Driving corridors:

Width at 300 yards: ~26 yards; narrower than TPC Twin Cities (~36), Deere Run (~36), Sawgrass (~28), Craig Ranch (~25–28), and broadly in line with Colonial/Muirfield/Harbour Town‑type precision tracks.

Tree‑lined Ross parkland, with bunkers clustered around 290–300; Bryson specifically noted “a lot of bunkers around like 290” and his intent to fly them, which illustrates the hazard‑at‑driver‑distance design.

Fairways expand in some strategic zones and constrict in others, explicitly to force decisions rather than let bomb‑and‑gauge be automatic.

Par‑5 reachability:

Both par‑5s (4, 14) are reachable for top‑end length; Davis repeatedly cites “playing the par 5s well obviously helps out here because they are reachable.”

Bryson speaks to having 9‑iron into par‑5s and “being the casino” by always attacking; Finau references a full‑blood 4‑iron into a par‑5 as a key momentum shot.

Mechanism: Players with high ball speed and the ability to clear or thread bunkers at 290–300 yards can transform par‑5s into mid‑iron scoring holes while still keeping driver in play on narrow corridors; short or spin‑inefficient drivers get squeezed.

Surfaces, Firmness & Short‑Game Demands
Surfaces and play characteristics:

Greens: pure bentgrass, USGA spec, expanded, with Ross contour restoration; speed targeted around stimp 11, but new surfaces are heavily watered and can play both receptive (for spinny high shots) and repelling (for marginal strike or wrong tier).

Rough: Tall fescue / bluegrass to ~4", enough to penalize wide misses without U.S. Open‑level ankle‑high brutality; Finau notes Detroit as “mentally forgiving” compared to US Open venues.

Fairways: Bentgrass, high‑quality surfaces, uneven lies typical of Ross, with meandering through trees and distinct sightlines; Bryson describes them as “perfect” country‑club style.

Short‑game stress:

Historical SG decomposition: winners from 2021‑25 gained ~2.65 strokes/round tee‑to‑green vs 0.88 putting, with Around‑the‑Green contributing ~0.60 strokes/round – notably high relative to generic PGA setups.

Tournament scrambling averages for winners sit around 31st in the field; Finau’s win showed a ceiling with 100% scrambling and #1 GIR, but most winners are in the mid‑pack scrambling ranks while leaning on elite ball‑striking.

Mechanism: Detroit rewards players who can (a) find correct tiers on large, contoured bent surfaces, and (b) handle Ross runoff areas and expanded bunker inventories with real short‑game craft; pure putters with weak tee‑to‑green profiles are structurally capped.

What Type of Golf Wins Here?
Winner Trait Pattern
Winner profile data:

Driving distance/accuracy:

Dist rank of winners: ~20th on average; multiple winners (DeChambeau 351 yards, Potgieter #1 in driving distance) are absolute bombers, others like Finau/Davis are strong modern drivers (~300–310).

Accuracy rank: ~31st on average; Finau and Lashley hit a lot of fairways, while Bryson/Fowler lived with lower accuracy and leveraged speed.

Approach/GIR:

GIR rank: ~10th on average; most winners are top‑10 in GIR, with Finau #1 and several winners top‑5 in SG Approach/Tee‑to‑Green.

Short game/putting:

Scrambling rank: ~31st; Putting average ~15th. Winners are generally positive but not dominant putters, which matches the SG pattern of tee‑to‑green dominance plus competent putting.

Stroke‑gained averages:

SG Off‑the‑Tee: ~19th

SG Approach: ~13th

SG Around‑the‑Green: ~15th

SG Tee‑to‑Green: ~3rd

SG Putting: ~15th

Mechanism: Detroit is structurally a Tee‑to‑Green first venue where winners consistently rank top‑5 in SG Tee‑to‑Green, combine strong but not necessarily elite putting, and often have above‑tour power.

Anti‑Patterns
Implied anti‑patterns from the venue and winner set:

Short, low‑speed drivers who rely on pure accuracy but cannot attack par‑5s or long par‑4s with mid‑iron. They can contend (Hadwin, Bhatia, Kisner, Morikawa, Snedeker have high finishes) but are structurally capped unless they have near‑perfect irons and putting.

Overly wild bombers who do not gain SG Off‑the‑Tee: Detroit’s 26‑yard corridors and OB/tree framing on 4, plus bunkers at 290, punish variance‑heavy drivers who can’t either shape or flight to zones.

One‑dimensional putters: Strong SG Putting alone without robust SG Approach/OTT tends not to win here; winners average only ~15th in SG Putting but ~3rd SG Tee‑to‑Green.

Trait list for winners/elite finishers:

Above‑average distance with usable accuracy (Finau, Davis, DeChambeau, Cantlay, Young, Potgieter).

Strong SG Tee‑to‑Green and GIR (Finau, Fowler, Davis, Lashley).

Comfortable on Ross and classic parkland (Davis cites loving Donald Ross layouts; Horschel, Reed, Snedeker have repeat Ross success).

Bentgrass‑positive putting, especially on contoured, back‑to‑front Ross greens.

2026 Weather & Conditions
10‑day Detroit forecast around tournament dates:

Thu Jul 30 (R1): Mostly clear, low ~64, high ~86, limited precipitation (~2%), light winds.

Fri Jul 31 (R2): Partly cloudy, low ~67, high ~86, small rain chance (~15%).

Sat Aug 1 (R3): Scattered showers, low ~66, high ~83, precip ~51%; potential thunderstorms risk flagged by Bamford as “almost automatic” under 86–90°F heat, especially Saturday.

Sun Aug 2 (R4): Partly cloudy, low ~64, high ~83, ~24% precip, light to moderate breezes.

Extended pattern:

Overall week: Bamford projects 30–32°C (86–90°F), hot and humid, with light wind “lighter than 2025” and thunderstorm risk particularly on Saturday.

Historical weather shows the event frequently plays in mid‑80s to low‑90s, with mixed cloud, occasional delays, and variable breezes 5–20 mph; 2025 had 87–90°F with SW/SSW winds and gusts into mid‑20s mph.

Wind/condition traits:

Light winds + heat increase ball speed and carry, which amplifies bomber advantage off the tee and into par‑5s, while making new greens more receptive if properly watered.

Thunderstorm risk introduces potential softening and delay; soft bent with big greens accentuates high‑launch, high‑spin iron players and strong putters, while reducing penalty for slightly off‑line drives in some spots.

Weather‑paired traits:

Heat‑fit, high‑speed drivers who can attack soft par‑5s and manage humidity fatigue.

Good wet‑weather iron players and those comfortable with bumpy greens later in the day; Fowler notes how soft Poa got “bumpy” before the bent renovation, but new large bent surfaces will still show footprint and spike marks under heavy traffic.

Wind‑adaptive shotmakers: past years with 10–20 mph winds rewarded players like Finau and Davis who can flight and shape; Reed explicitly calls Detroit a “shot‑making golf course” where you must use contours and wind.

Implications: With 2026 projected as hot, humid, relatively low‑wind with Saturday storm risk, expect:

Scoring pushing toward Bamford’s −20 expectation;

Slightly enhanced bomber edge;

Afternoon waves dealing with more green bumpiness and potential delays.

